INT. CHEESE CAVE - DAWN

A cool limestone vault lined with shelves of wheels, rinds blooming blue. A single dripping spigot. The air is thick with ammonia and earth.

GASTON (60s, rubber apron) crouches over an overturned wheel. HELOISE (30s, health inspector, clipboard) holds a penlight. IGNATIUS (50s, owner's cousin, silk scarf) hovers. JOSEPHINE (20s, apprentice, smock) fills a pail.

Gaston turns the wheel over. A man's wristwatch is baked into the rind.

HELOISE
Is that normal?

GASTON
Not for this cheese.

IGNATIUS
Heloise, we should call the owner.

HELOISE
The owner is missing.

Ignatius flinches. Josephine sets the pail down very, very carefully.

JOSEPHINE
I ought to wash my hands.

HELOISE
Stay where you are.

Josephine laughs, too high, too quick. Gaston stands. Sniffs the air. His eyes go to the far wall, to the oldest shelf, the one nobody touches.

GASTON
Ignatius. When did your cousin last visit?

IGNATIUS
Tuesday.

GASTON
And you were here.

IGNATIUS
No. I mean yes. I mean I was only passing.

Heloise shines her light on the old shelf. A boot protrudes from the rind of the largest wheel.
